  "_style": "Address the user warmly and directly with singular “wena” (verbs in the 2nd person singular: u-, -akho, “Gcina”, “Ubhale ileta”), and the couple together with plural “nina / nobabini” (“ni-”, “-enu”, “Umthi wenu”, “nobabini”); when the user speaks to their partner (messages, letters) use “mna/ndi-”, and for things the two share use “thina/wethu” (“Umthi wethu”). The other person is always “iqabane lakho” (or just “iqabane”); never use “indoda/umfazi/isithandwa sam/inkwenkwe/intombi”, and since Xhosa pronouns and verb agreement are not gendered, keep sentences neutral by agreeing with the noun class of “iqabane” (class 5: “lona”, “li-”) or with the name, never adding gendered words. Quote marks: curly “ ” outside and ‘ ’ inside; single ellipsis character …; no space before ? ! : ;, one space after; keep “ · ”, \\n, emoji and every placeholder (%@, %lld, %1$@) exactly. A partner's name placeholder (%@) is never prefixed or declined (no “ku-%@”, “ka-%@”, “no-%@”, “ngu-%@”): rephrase so the name stands alone, e.g. “Iqabane lakho, %@, …” or put the name at the start/after a colon. Write numbers with Western digits, a space as thousands separator and a comma for decimals (1 250, 4,5); dates follow the system formatter (day month year, e.g. “14 Novemba 2026”), never hand-built; months and weekdays in Xhosa lower case inside sentences. Buttons and actions are short imperatives (“Gcina”, “Thumela ileta”, “Zama kwakhona”) with no “nceda/please”; titles, tabs and buttons use sentence case (capital on the first letter only), except proper names like Coupley, Premium, AI, CoupleSync. Latin script, standard isiXhosa orthography (izihlanganisi like “we-AI”, “nge-%@” only on fixed words, never on names). Plural categories are [\"one\",\"other\"]: “one” singular (“%lld usuku”), “other” plural (“%lld iintsuku”); any extra category copies “other”. Tone: warm, light, kind and natural like good Xhosa app text — never cheesy, never word-for-word from English.",
